{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: dap-family note draft 2 at 9ef86270 (artifact 96f9171c), notes/2026-11-23-dap-family.md, against main b0a6439a. This answers request b0b1ad8e, which replaces 3b7303a4.\n\nI read the whole of draft 2 and checked each answer to verdict 7e5a904e against the code at the head.\n\n- B1, B2 fixed. `offer-claim` names the claimant, and `accept-offer` guards `signer: [\"claimant\"]`. docs/scopes.md defines `signer` as \"the signer is in one of the listed party slots of the subject\", so only the named member can accept. No secret or hash reaches the person scope.\n- B3 fixed. Section 5.3 matches docs/scopes.md (Relationship updates): copies come from children and couples only, the copy's state belongs to the owner, and an answer is a local `link-answer` item. G12 records confirmation by relatives as a gap.\n- B4 fixed. Section 6.1 names the slots that are detached for every person. Section 8.4 lists what stays in the journals. The `create` message carries no name or date.\n- M1 fixed. The merge runs as ordinary acts under one custody (6.5), with G8.\n- M2 fixed. `ResultClauses` has `refused` (definition.ts:271), so the clause that sets `copied` to false is expressible. `scopeRecords` is 200 000 (bounds.ts:124), as stated.\n- M3, M4 fixed. `set-visibility` reads only `living`. `member-of` and `unmerge` are gone, and section 12 agrees with section 5.\n\nNon-blocking points for the next draft or the F0 tests:\n\nN1. `add-person` takes its subject and stewards from the signer of the circle act (3.3 step 4, 5.2). Section 6.3 states no guard, so a member could name another member as the subject of a new record without that member's claim. Add a guard: the subject is the signer or absent.\n\nN2. The living-event rule binds only when an event is added. A record that `set-living` moves from `deceased` to `living` keeps its exact dates and places in the journal. Refuse that move while exact events stand, or say that a redaction cannot remove them. The default `living` value of `unknown` also strips places from every imported ancestor until a steward marks them deceased. Say how a GEDCOM import (F2) sets `living`.\n\nN3. The circle's `invitation` has the state `redeemed`, but no message tells the circle about `accept-offer` or `join`. Say who signs the move, or drop the state.\n\nConclusions\n\nArchitecture: sound. One scope for each person, parentage owned by the child, no fan-in on the write path, copies used only as docs/scopes.md defines them, and projections without authority.\n\nSecurity: acceptable for a design note. Only the named member can accept a bound claim, and no secret crosses scopes. No reader without a key reaches a scope. N1 is a missing guard, not a design fault.\n\nSimplification: done. The simplifications I proposed are all applied: `edit-*` removed, four claim states, `family-merge` deferred to F6. I see nothing further to cut.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:96f9171cd01bfb0976285d1ef13661a81960f58b","binding":"assigned","head":"9ef86270b48ce51569e82b8cac48d440e3d4536e","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:798cd6c3333b4312245fffe25e78e21bad9d6ce1\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:aa85bdaf0f256e390ffd92b082bf16a71a0b123d\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:798cd6c3333b4312245fffe25e78e21bad9d6ce1","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}